<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edo (Tokyo)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edo, renamed Tokyo in 1868, was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> made it his base, and it grew into one of the largest cities in the world, partly because the alternate attendance system required <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> to spend time there. It remained the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s government until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>.</p></div>
